// 26 - window_shadow        upstream: tools/upstream/examples/window_shadow.rs (227 lines)
//
// ON macOS THIS UPSTREAM EXAMPLE IS MOSTLY DEAD — read this before comparing the
// two windows, because what you see below is upstream's *real* macOS behaviour,
// not a truncated port.
//
// Upstream asks for a client-decorated window
// (`window_decorations: Some(WindowDecorations::Client)`) and then branches its
// whole tree on `window.window_decorations()`. On macOS that branch never runs,
// and that is upstream's own source, not a limit of this port:
//
//   * `PlatformWindow::window_decorations` defaults to `Decorations::Server`
//     (gpui-0.2.2 src/platform.rs:538), and the macOS implementation —
//     `impl PlatformWindow for MacWindow`, src/platform/mac/window.rs:953 —
//     does not override it. `Window::window_decorations` merely forwards
//     (src/window.rs:1769), so it answers `Server` and the
//     `Decorations::Client { .. }` arm is unreachable.
//   * That same macOS impl block overrides none of `set_client_inset`
//     (src/platform.rs:548), `start_window_resize` (:537), `start_window_move`
//     (:536), `show_window_menu` (:535) or `request_decorations` (:534). The
//     first four are no-op defaults there, and only the Linux Wayland/X11
//     backends implement them — so even the calls upstream makes
//     unconditionally do nothing on macOS.
//
// So what macOS actually draws for `window_shadow.rs` is the `Server` arm, and
// that is exactly the tree below. Upstream's client-drawn window shadow, its
// 10px client inset, its resize band, its drag-to-move, its right-click window
// menu and its "this is the custom titlebar" text all live inside the dead arm
// and are not on screen. The window keeps its normal macOS title bar and the
// shadow the OS draws around the frame; what remains of the tree is a #CCCCFF
// panel holding a 300x300 white card and a 200x100 green box, both carrying
// GPUI shadows drawn as ordinary elements — `shadow=lg` on the card and a
// custom 20px black `BoxShadow` on the green box, the two shadow specs that do
// execute here. The #CCCCFF panel keeps upstream's `cursor(CursorStyle::Arrow)`
// (spelled `cursor=default`), which is inert anyway — the arrow is GPUI's
// default cursor, and the binding only gives a plain box a hitbox when it also
// carries `hover=`/`active=` or scrolling — and the inner `on_mouse_move(..)`
// that called `cx.stop_propagation()` has nothing left to stop.
//
// Because that leaves almost nothing to see, the port also *approximates* the
// pointer work the dead arm would have done, using the binding's pointer API:
// a small readout panel (this port's addition, not upstream's) printing
// `gpui::mouse_position()` and the edge `resize_edge()` picks — the computation
// upstream feeds to `set_cursor_style` and `start_window_resize` — and an
// `on_mouse` on the green box, standing in for upstream's `on_mouse_down` /
// `on_click` handlers there.
//
// Deviations:
//
//   * The `Decorations::Client` arm is omitted, not truncated: it cannot run on
//     macOS (above). Nothing that runs on macOS is missing.
//   * `window.set_client_inset(shadow_size)` — not exposed by the binding and a
//     no-op on macOS (src/platform.rs:548). Upstream's 10px inset never exists.
//   * `window.insert_hitbox` / `HitboxBehavior` — not exposed. Upstream uses a
//     full-window hitbox as the target of its per-edge resize cursor, so there
//     is no `set_cursor_style` here; the readout prints the edge instead. The
//     style language's `cursor=` is a static refinement, and the binding only
//     gives a plain box a hitbox when it also carries `hover=`/`active=` or
//     scrolling (gpui-binding/src/view.rs:579-591), so a per-frame cursor token
//     could not take effect either.
//   * `window.start_window_resize` / `start_window_move` / `show_window_menu` —
//     not exposed, and no-ops on macOS anyway. The readout names the call
//     upstream would have made, and `on_mouse("down up move right", "hello")`
//     on the green box stands in for upstream's `on_mouse_down(Left) ->
//     start_window_move()` and its `on_click` + `is_right_click() ->
//     show_window_menu(..)` (the binding's `right` kind is a right-button
//     press).
//   * `window.refresh()` — not exposed. Upstream repaints on every mouse move
//     (`on_mouse_move(|_e, window, _| window.refresh())`, which is in the dead
//     arm); with nothing to trigger a frame, `mouse_position()` would freeze,
//     so the port calls `gpui::animate()` and repaints every frame, as
//     01_animation does.
//   * `cx.observe_window_appearance(..)` — not exposed; it only called
//     `window.refresh()` on an appearance change, which the frame loop covers.
//   * `cx.stop_propagation()` — not exposed. No ancestor mouse handler survives
//     in the `Server` arm, so it has nothing to stop.
//   * `gpui::mouse_position()` is one frame stale: the binding reads it before
//     the view callback runs, because a binding function has no `Window`.
//     Upstream reads it during paint, inside its canvas closure.
//   * `window.window_bounds().get_bounds().size` — no window size is exposed,
//     so `resize_edge()` is handed the requested 600x600. Resizing the window
//     moves upstream's band but not this readout's, and upstream's `size` is
//     its window bounds where 600x600 is the content size.
//   * `on_mouse`'s `move` kind fires only while a button is held — the binding
//     requires `event.dragging()` (gpui-binding/src/view.rs:905-913) — so the
//     hover readout comes from `mouse_position()`, not from `on_mouse`.
//   * `on_mouse` renders its element inside a wrapper that carries no style of
//     its own (`Kind::Mouse`, gpui-binding/src/view.rs:863-915), so a wrapped
//     `size=full` box has no definite height to resolve against; the port
//     attaches `on_mouse` only to the green box, whose 200x100 size is fixed.
//   * `id("window-backdrop")` and `id("hello")` — element ids belong to the
//     binding.
//   * Upstream's `&&` in `resize_edge` is written out as nested `if`s; the
//     tests and their order are the same.
//   * Colours are exact here: the green box's `BoxShadow` is
//     `Hsla { h: 0., s: 0., l: 0., a: 1.0 }`, which is `#000000ff` with no
//     rounding, and the card's shadow is GPUI's own `shadow=lg` preset.
//     `transparent_black()` is `bg=transparent`.
//   * `div()` becomes `ui::rect`/`ui::panel`/`ui::column`/`ui::row`, and as in
//     every port the frame is rebuilt from scratch rather than re-rendering a
//     retained `Entity<WindowShadow>`.

use gpui
use std::process
use ui

/// Upstream's `shadow_size = px(10.0)` — the client inset, and the width of the
/// resize band the dead arm tests against.
const SHADOW: f64 = 10.0

/// Upstream's window: `Bounds::centered(None, size(px(600.0), px(600.0)), cx)`.
const WINDOW: f64 = 600.0

// ---- the dead arm's decision, kept visible ----------------------------

/// Upstream's `resize_edge`, answering the edge's name instead of a
/// `ResizeEdge`; `"none"` is upstream's `None`.
fn resize_edge(x: f64, y: f64, shadow: f64, w: f64, h: f64) -> String {
    if y < shadow {
        if x < shadow {
            "TopLeft"
        } else if x > w - shadow {
            "TopRight"
        } else {
            "Top"
        }
    } else if y > h - shadow {
        if x < shadow {
            "BottomLeft"
        } else if x > w - shadow {
            "BottomRight"
        } else {
            "Bottom"
        }
    } else if x < shadow {
        "Left"
    } else if x > w - shadow {
        "Right"
    } else {
        "none"
    }
}

/// What upstream's `on_mouse_down` on the backdrop would call for a pointer at
/// this edge: `start_window_resize(edge)` on one, `start_window_move()` off
/// them. Neither is exposed, and on macOS both are no-ops.
fn press_note(edge: String) -> String {
    if edge == "none" {
        "start_window_move()"
    } else {
        f"start_window_resize({edge})"
    }
}

/// The same for the green box, whose client-arm handlers upstream wires to
/// `on_mouse_down(Left) -> start_window_move()` and to `on_click` +
/// `is_right_click() -> show_window_menu(..)`. `kind` is the box's last
/// pointer event; `x`/`y` are relative to its corner, as `on_mouse` reports.
fn titlebar_note(kind: String, x: f64, y: f64) -> String {
    match kind {
        "down" => "left press -> start_window_move()",
        "right" => "right press -> show_window_menu(..)",
        "move" => f"dragging at {x:.0}, {y:.0}",
        "up" => "left release",
        _ => "quiet",
    }
}

// ---- the readout (this port's addition) -------------------------------

/// The pointer the binding can see, and the decision the dead arm would have
/// taken from it. Upstream draws nothing like this; it is here so the example
/// shows something that follows the pointer on macOS.
fn readout(mx: f64, my: f64, edge: String, press: String, titlebar: String) -> ui::El {
    ui::column("absolute top=8 left=8 p=8 rounded=6 gap=2 bg=#000000cc color=#ffffff font=sm")
        .text("client-decoration arm, approximated", "color=#a6adc8")
        .text(f"mouse_position()  {mx:.0}, {my:.0}", "")
        .text(f"resize_edge()     {edge}", "")
        .text(f"press would       {press}", "")
        .text(f"green box         {titlebar}", "")
}

fn main() {
    let outcome = gpui::run("26 - window_shadow", WINDOW, WINDOW, || {
        // Stands in for upstream's `on_mouse_move(..) -> window.refresh()`:
        // repaint every frame so `mouse_position()` tracks the pointer.
        gpui::animate()

        for event in gpui::take_events() {
            let id, kind = gpui::event_parts(event)
            if id == "hello" {
                gpui::set_text("hello.last", kind)
            }
        }

        let mx, my = gpui::mouse_position()
        let edge = resize_edge(mx, my, SHADOW, WINDOW, WINDOW)
        let press = press_note(edge)
        let last = gpui::state_text("hello.last", "")
        let drag_x = gpui::state_float("hello.x", 0.0)
        let drag_y = gpui::state_float("hello.y", 0.0)
        let titlebar = titlebar_note(last, drag_x, drag_y)

        // Upstream's tree in the `Decorations::Server` arm, which is the arm
        // macOS takes: root, the #CCCCFF panel, the white card, the green box.
        ui::panel("size=full bg=transparent")
            .child(
                ui::column("bg=#ccccff size=full justify=around cursor=default")
                    .child(ui::row("w=full justify=around")
                        .child(ui::panel("flex size=300 bg=white justify=center items=center shadow=lg border=1 bordercolor=#0000ff font=xl color=#ffffff")
                            .child(ui::rect("w=200 h=100 bg=green shadow=#000000ff,0,0,20,0")
                                .on_mouse("down up move right", "hello")))))
            .child(readout(mx, my, edge, press, titlebar))
            |> ui::root
    })

    if let Err(problem) = outcome {
        eprintln(f"gpui::run stopped with an error: {problem}")
        process::exit(1)
    }
}
